curity that helps to detect and mitigate certain types of attacks, including Cross Site Scripting (XSS) and data injection attacks. These attacks are used for everything from data theft to site defacement or distribution of malware. CSP provides a set of standard HTTP headers that allow website owners to declare approved sources of content that browsers should be allowed to load on that page — covered types are JavaScript, CSS, HTML frames, fonts, images and embeddable objects such as Java applets, ActiveX, audio and video files.','Ensure that your web server, application server, load balancer, etc. is configured to set the Content-Security-Policy header.','OWASP_ZAP','OBSERVED','2026-10-29 06:35:45.446861','10038','High','https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Guides/CSP\nhttps://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Content_Security_Policy_Cheat_Sheet.html\nhttps://www.w3.org/TR/CSP/\nhttps://w3c.github.io/webappsec-csp/\nhttps://web.dev/articles/csp\nhttps://caniuse.com/#feat=contentsecuritypolicy\nhttps://content-security-policy.com/','693','15'),(1379,48,'INFORMATION_DISCLOSURE_SERVER','LOW','http://testaspnet.vulnweb.com/sitemap.xml','Microsoft-IIS/8.5','The web/application server is leaking version information via the \"Server\" HTTP response header. Access to such information may facilitate attackers identifying other vulnerabilities your web/application server is subject to.','Ensure that your web server, application server, load balancer, etc. is configured to suppress the \"Server\" header or provide generic details.','OWASP_ZAP','OBSERVED','2026-10-29 06:35:45.461860','10036','High','https://httpd.apache.org/docs/current/mod/core.html#servertokens\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/previous-versions/msp-n-p/ff648552(v=pandp.10)\nhttps://www.troyhunt.com/shhh-dont-let-your-response-headers/','497','13'),(1380,48,'INFORMATION_DISCLOSURE_SERVER','LOW','http://testaspnet.vulnweb.com/sitemap.xml','X-Powered-By: ASP.NET','The web/application server is leaking information via one or more \"X-Powered-By\" HTTP response headers. Access to such information may facilitate attackers identifying other frameworks/components your web application is reliant upon and the vulnerabilities such components may be subject to.','Ensure that your web server, application server, load balancer, etc. is configured to suppress \"X-Powered-By\" headers.','OWASP_ZAP','OBSERVED','2026-10-29 06:35:45.490905','10037','Medium','https://owasp.org/www-project-web-security-testing-guide/v42/4-Web_Application_Security_Testing/01-Information_Gathering/08-Fingerprint_Web_Application_Framework\nhttps://www.troyhunt.com/shhh-dont-let-your-response-headers/','497','13'),(1381,48,'ZAP_ZAP_IS_OUT_OF_DATE','LOW','http://testaspnet.vulnweb.com/sitemap.xml','ZAP is Out of Date','The version of ZAP you are using to test your app is out of date and is no longer being updated.\nThe risk level is set based on how out of date your ZAP version is.','Download the latest version of ZAP from https://www.zaproxy.org/download/ and install it.','OWASP_ZAP','POTENTIAL','2026-10-29 06:35:45.504827','10116','High','https://www.zaproxy.org/download/','1104','45'),(1382,48,'INFORMATION_DISCLOSURE_SERVER','LOW','http://testaspnet.vulnweb.com/images/logo_acunetix.gif','Microsoft-IIS/8.5','The web/application server is leaking version information via the \"Server\" HTTP response header. Access to such information may facilitate attackers identifying other vulnerabilities your web/application server is subject to.','Ensure that your web server, application server, load balancer, etc. is configured to suppress the \"Server\" header or provide generic details.','OWASP_ZAP','OBSERVED','2026-10-29 06:35:45.513749','10036','High','https://httpd.apache.org/docs/current/mod/core.html#servertokens\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/previous-versions/msp-n-p/ff648552(v=pandp.10)\nhttps://www.troyhunt.com/shhh-dont-let-your-response-headers/','497','13'),(1383,48,'INFORMATION_DISCLOSURE_SERVER','LOW','http://testaspnet.vulnweb.com/robots.txt','Microsoft-IIS/8.5','The web/application server is leaking version information via the \"Server\" HTTP response header. Access to such information may facilitate attackers identifying other vulnerabilities your web/application server is subject to.','Ensure that your web server, application server, load balancer, etc. is configured to suppress the \"Server\" header or provide generic details.','OWASP_ZAP','OBSERVED','2026-10-29 06:35:45.524459','10036','High','https://httpd.apache.org/docs/current/mod/core.html#servertokens\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/previous-versions/msp-n-p/ff648552(v=pandp.10)\nhttps://www.troyhunt.com/shhh-dont-let-your-response-headers/','497','13'),(1384,48,'MISSING_CONTENT_TYPE_OPTIONS','LOW','http://testaspnet.vulnweb.com/images/logo_acunetix.gif [x-content-type-options]','X-Content-Type-Options Header Missing','The Anti-MIME-Sniffing header X-Content-Type-Options was not set to \'nosniff\'. This allows older versions of Internet Explorer and Chrome to perform MIME-sniffing on the response body, potentially causing the response body to be interpreted and displayed as a content type other than the declared content type. Current (early 2014) and legacy versions of Firefox will use the declared content type (if one is set), rather than performing MIME-sniffing.','Ensure that the application/web server sets the Content-Type header appropriately, and that it sets the X-Content-Type-Options header to \'nosniff\' for all web pages.\nIf possible, ensure that the end user uses a standards-compliant and modern web browser that does not perform MIME-sniffing at all, or that can be directed by the web application/web server to not perform MIME-sniffing.','OWASP_ZAP','OBSERVED','2026-10-29 06:35:45.532831','10021','Medium','https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/previous-versions/windows/internet-explorer/ie-developer/compatibility/gg622941(v=vs.85)\nhttps://owasp.org/www-community/Security_Headers','693','15'),(1385,48,'MISSING_CONTENT_TYPE_OPTIONS','LOW','http://testaspnet.vulnweb.com/robots.txt [x-content-type-options]','X-Content-Type-Options Header Missing','The Anti-MIME-Sniffing header X-Content-Type-Options was not set to \'nosniff\'. This allows older versions of Internet Explorer and Chrome to perform MIME-sniffing on the response body, potentially causing the response body to be interpreted and displayed as a content type other than the declared content type. Current (early 2014) and legacy versions of Firefox will use the declared content type (if one is set), rather than performing MIME-sniffing.','Ensure that the application/web server sets the Content-Type header appropriately, and that it sets the X-Content-Type-Options header to \'nosniff\' for all web pages.\nIf possible, ensure that the end user uses a standards-compliant and modern web browser that does not perform MIME-sniffing at all, or that can be directed by the web application/web server to not perform MIME-sniffing.','OWASP_ZAP','OBSERVED','2026-10-29 06:35:45.541812','10021','Medium','https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/previous-versions/windows/internet-explorer/ie-developer/compatibility/gg622941(v=vs.85)\nhttps://owasp.org/www-community/Security_Headers','693','15'),(1386,48,'INFORMATION_DISCLOSURE_SERVER','LOW','http://testaspnet.vulnweb.com/images/logo_acunetix.gif','X-Powered-By: ASP.NET','The web/application server is leaking information via one or more \"X-Powered-By\" HTTP response headers. Access to such information may facilitate attackers identifying other frameworks/components your web application is reliant upon and the vulnerabilities such components may be subject to.','Ensure that your web server, application server, load balancer, etc. is configured to suppress \"X-Powered-By\" headers.','OWASP_ZAP','OBSERVED','2026-10-29 06:35:45.552695','10037','Medium','https://owasp.org/www-project-web-security-testing-guide/v42/4-Web_Application_Security_Testing/01-Information_Gathering/08-Fingerprint_Web_Application_Framework\nhttps://www.troyhunt.com/shhh-dont-let-your-response-headers/','497','13'),(1387,48,'INFORMATION_DISCLOSURE_SERVER','LOW','http://testaspnet.vulnweb.com/robots.txt','X-Powered-By: ASP.NET','The web/application server is leaking information via one or more \"X-Powered-By\" HTTP response headers. Access to such information may facilitate attackers identifying other frameworks/components your web application is reliant upon and the vulnerabilities such components may be subject to.','Ensure that your web server, application server, load balancer, etc. is configured to suppress \"X-Powered-By\" headers.','OWASP_ZAP','OBSERVED','2026-10-29 06:35:45.560643','10037','Medium','https://owasp.org/www-project-web-security-testing-guide/v42/4-Web_Application_Security_Testing/01-Information_Gathering/08-Fingerprint_Web_Application_Framework\nhttps://www.troyhunt.com/shhh-dont-let-your-response-headers/','497','13'),(1388,48,'MISSING_FRAME_PROTECTION','MEDIUM','http://testaspnet.vulnweb.com/login.aspx [x-frame-options]','Missing Anti-clickjacking Header','The response does not protect against \'ClickJacking\' attacks. It should include either Content-Security-Policy with \'frame-ancestors\' directive or X-Frame-Options.','Modern Web browsers support the Content-Security-Policy and X-Frame-Options HTTP headers. Ensure one of them is set on all web pages returned by your site/app.\nIf you expect the page to be framed only by pages on your server (e.g. it\'s part of a FRAMESET) then you\'ll want to use SAMEORIGIN, otherwise if you never expect the page to be framed, you should use DENY. Alternatively consider implementing Content Security Policy\'s \"frame-ancestors\" directive.','OWASP_ZAP','OBSERVED','2026-10-29 06:35:45.575137','10020','Medium','https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Reference/Headers/X-Frame-Options','1021','15'),(1389,48,'INFORMATION_DISCLOSURE_SERVER','LOW','http://testaspnet.vulnweb.com/